---
import CaseStudy from "~/layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import CaseHero from "~/components/case/CaseHero.astro";
import CaseLead from "~/components/case/CaseLead.astro";
import Hood from "~/components/case/Hood.astro";
import NextNav from "~/components/case/NextNav.astro";
import Scenes from "~/components/case/Scenes.astro";
import SceneStep from "~/components/case/SceneStep.astro";
import Section from "~/components/ui/Section.astro";

const actions = [
  {
    name: "pulumi-preview",
    what: "runs the tests and the preview with the policies enforced, saves the plan and posts it on the pull request",
  },
  { name: "pulumi-deploy", what: "fetches the approved plan, checks for drift and applies only that plan" },
  { name: "pulumi-drift", what: "a scheduled check that what’s running still matches the code" },
  { name: "setup-pulumi", what: "the toolchain, with the Pulumi CLI pinned to the version in the lockfile" },
  { name: "pulumi-plan-store", what: "moves saved plans to and from S3" },
  { name: "slack-notify", what: "one status message per run, including failures before Pulumi starts" },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  project="infrastructure-delivery"
  title="Infrastructure delivery · Guus Ekkelenkamp"
  og="infrastructure-delivery"
  description="Case study: how an infrastructure change at Harborn is reviewed, checked by policy and applied by the pipeline, as new infrastructure moves to Pulumi."
>
  <CaseHero
    project="infrastructure-delivery"
    meta="Work at Harborn · 2026 to now · I designed the pipeline and built the shared actions and the CLI"
    title="Infrastructure delivery"
    line="How a change to our infrastructure is reviewed, checked and applied, as new infrastructure moves to Pulumi."
  />
  <CaseLead>
    <p>
      The <a href="/projects/hosting-platform/">hosting platform</a> runs more than 300 clients with over 800
      deployments, so one bad infrastructure change can reach a lot of them at once. Its clusters come from
      Terraform and OpenTofu modules. New infrastructure is Pulumi in Python.
    </p>
    <p>
      I set up how that Pulumi code reaches production. It started as a proof of concept that deployed one
      small VPC, because the pipeline was what I wanted to test, not the VPC. From there I built the shared
      GitHub Actions our Pulumi projects deploy through, and haven, the CLI the team uses to work in these
      environments.
    </p>
  </CaseLead>
  <Scenes id="story" title="How a change reaches production">
    <SceneStep scene="plan" range={[0, 0.5]} caption="A change from pull request to production.">
      <h3>The plan is what gets approved</h3>
      <p>
        A pull request runs the tests and a preview with the policies enforced. The preview saves the exact
        plan and posts it as a comment. The approval is a review by someone other than the author, of that
        plan.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="plan" range={[0.5, 1]} continues>
      <h3>The pipeline applies only that plan</h3>
      <p>
        On merge the deploy fetches the saved plan, refreshes production and works the plan out again. If
        production has gained a create, delete or replace that nobody reviewed, the deploy stops and changes
        nothing. We fix the drift and run the pull request again, so a change made by hand shows up as a
        failure instead of being reverted without anyone seeing it.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="policy">
      <h3>The policy comes before the resource</h3>
      <p>
        Each Pulumi project has a CrossGuard policy pack with mandatory enforcement, so a resource that breaks
        a rule can’t be previewed or applied. A new kind of resource starts with its rule and a test for it,
        and only then the resource. An exception is a change to the policy, with its reason in the pull
        request.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our log bucket is the example. Loki’s compactor handles log retention, because it tracks which chunks
        the index still uses. An S3 expiry rule on that bucket would delete chunks the index still points at,
        and queries over that period would return incomplete results without an error. The rule looks like
        sensible cost control, which is why a reviewer would approve it, so a policy rejects it before the
        pull request can be merged.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="writer" range={[0, 0.5]}>
      <h3>Every environment has its own foundation</h3>
      <p>
        Each environment has its own state bucket, encryption key and deploy role, so a deploy to acceptance
        can’t touch production, and a lost state file costs one subsystem instead of the platform. These roles
        are created outside Pulumi, and the deploy role can’t change its own permissions, so the pipeline can
        never give itself more rights.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
    <SceneStep scene="writer" range={[0.5, 1]} continues>
      <h3>A CLI without an apply command</h3>
      <p>
        I built haven, the CLI we use for these environments. It logs you in through SSO with an admin or a
        read-only role, opens a shell on the bastion, runs the policy checks and the tests, and sets up a new
        environment’s foundation, including its repository variables and branch protection on GitHub. Commands
        that change something only run from that environment’s own directory. There’s no apply or deploy
        command, because production only changes through the pipeline.
      </p>
    </SceneStep>
  </Scenes>
  <Section id="actions" title="The shared actions">
    <Hood items={actions} />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A project’s workflows only check out the code, set up Pulumi, get credentials and call one of these
        actions. The logic is in the actions, versioned with a <code>v1</code> tag, so a fix reaches every
        project that uses them. There are templates for AWS and Azure, for pull requests, deploys, drift
        checks and a manual plan and apply.
      </p>
      <p>
        CI holds no cloud keys. Every job gets short-lived credentials through GitHub OIDC. Any pull request
        can assume the read-only role the previews use, so that role can read metadata but not every bucket,
        secret or log group.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>
  <Section id="small-changes" title="Small pull requests and serial applies" bodyClass="prose">
    <p>
      Because the review is the approval, a pull request has one concern. A refactor doesn’t ship with a new
      resource, and foundation and IAM changes get pull requests of their own, so the plan in the comment is
      short enough to read before you approve it. Two applies to the same stack never run at the same time.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some changes have no diff in git, such as a new machine image. For those there’s a manual plan and
      apply. Someone with write access reads the saved plan and starts the apply, which goes through the same
      drift check. That’s weaker than a review by someone other than the author, and our docs say so.
    </p>
    <p>
      The clusters still change through Terraform and OpenTofu. A Kubernetes upgrade goes one minor version at
      a time, after the upgrade checks pass and the plan is empty, so the upgrade plan holds nothing else. The
      nodes then roll one at a time. App releases go through Argo CD, which the
      <a href="/projects/hosting-platform/#hood">hosting platform</a> page covers.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section id="lessons" title="Lessons" bodyClass="prose">
    <p>
      My first version had a separate approval at deploy time, after the merge. It couldn’t guarantee that
      what was applied matched what had been previewed, and it held a runner while it waited. I removed it and
      made the pull request review the approval.
    </p>
    <p>
      The first deploy check also stopped deploys nobody had a problem with, because of fields the cloud fills
      in itself, like EKS add-on versions. Now it stops only on creates, deletes and replacements that weren’t
      in the approved plan, and the scheduled drift check reports the rest to Slack.
    </p>
    <p>
      For apps we use Argo CD, which pulls changes from git by itself. For infrastructure I chose a pipeline
      that pushes them, because every change has to stop at a human review, and an operator that reconciles on
      its own works against that.
    </p>
    <p>
      Separate foundations cost something. Every new state needs its own bootstrap and its own five workflows,
      and haven sets generic GitHub variable names, so in a repository with several states we set the
      state-specific ones by hand until haven supports that.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <NextNav
    prev={{
      href: "/projects/hosting-platform/",
      small: "Previous project",
      label: "Hosting platform",
      arrow: "left",
    }}
    next={{ href: "/projects/homelab/", small: "Next project", label: "Homelab", arrow: "right" }}
  />
</CaseStudy>
